# Regression test for a transpiler bug found migrating `boring/breakout.br`'s
# ball-spawning code: bare `Type(args)` construction of a genuinely external/opaque
# type used to unconditionally rewrite to `Type::new(args)`, even when the real type
# is a plain tuple struct with no inherent `new()` (e.g. bevy's `Mesh2d`/
# `MeshMaterial2d`) -- `cargo build` then fails with E0599 ("no function or associated
# item named `new`"), even though `boring build`'s own `--emit-rust` check reports
# success. `ext_tuple.Mesh2d`/`ext_tuple.MeshMaterial2d` (tests/cases/fixtures/ext_tuple)
# stand in for the real bevy types -- see that crate's own doc comment. This is a
# `transpile_project_test!` case specifically so a real `cargo run` exercises the
# generated Rust, not just a transpile-and-diff -- see `Transpiler::
# KNOWN_EXTERNAL_TUPLE_STRUCTS`'s doc in src/transpiler/mod.rs for the fix itself.
#
# `mat`'s explicit `MeshMaterial2d<int>` annotation sidesteps an unrelated, pre-existing
# transpiler bug: a local `let` with no annotation of its own that initializes from a
# `Type(args)` call picks up an inferred-qualifier type annotation (emit_let.rs's
# `compute_let_ty_and_value`, priority-5 branch) built from the bare callee name alone,
# dropping any generic parameters -- `let mat = MeshMaterial2d(7)` would mis-emit as
# `let mat: MeshMaterial2d = MeshMaterial2d(7);` (E0107, missing generics). Out of scope
# for this fix; tracked separately.
use ext_tuple.Mesh2d
use ext_tuple.MeshMaterial2d

def main():
    let mesh = Mesh2d(42)
    let MeshMaterial2d<int> mat = MeshMaterial2d(7)
    print "{mesh.0}"
    print "{mat.0}"
